From: Leonid Komarianskyi <leonid_komarianskyi@epam.com>
Subject: xen/arm: Fix size underflow in invalidate_dcache_va_range()

When the start address is not cacheline aligned, the bytes covered by
the first, partial cacheline are subtracted from 'size'. Nothing checks
that the region actually extends beyond that cacheline, so if it does
not, the unsigned subtraction underflows and the loop that follows walks
practically the entire address space.

For example, with a 64-byte cacheline, p = 0x40000001 and size = 1:

    p & cacheline_mask     -> 1, so the partial head is taken
    size -= 64 - 1         -> 1 - 63 underflows to 0xffffffffffffffc2

Instead of a single "dc civac" at 0x40000000, the loop then runs
288230376151711743 times, issuing "dc ivac" from 0x40000040 upwards.
Invalidating without cleaning discards dirty cachelines, so this
corrupts whatever is mapped along the way until Xen takes a data abort.

Moreover, _cache_flush() in common/grant_table.c, reachable from a guest
via GNTTABOP_cache_flush, bounds 'offset' and 'length' against the page
size but requires no alignment, so GNTTAB_CACHE_INVAL with offset = 1
and length = 1 is enough to trigger this. Note that the page may belong
to a different domain (the d != owner path), so the data being discarded
is not necessarily the caller's own.

The subtraction dates back to the fix for XSA-437, so this is a regression
in that change.

Fix this by detecting the underflow instead of letting it happen: if the
region ends inside the first cacheline, there is nothing left to flush
and the function bails out right after cleaning it. Partially covered
cachelines are still cleaned as well as invalidated, so that data outside
of the region is not discarded.

Additionally, drop 'idx' and advance 'p' directly instead. It simplifies
the logic while using fewer variables and less arithmetic.

Note that the partial head is now cleaned using the unaligned start of the
region instead of the start of its cacheline. This is permitted by the
architecture; the Arm ARM (DDI 0487) states:

  "When a DC instruction requires an address argument this takes the form
  of a 64-bit register that holds the VA argument. No alignment
  restrictions apply for this address."

The same applies on Arm32.

Also, switch the asm() in the loop to asm_inline, like the neighbouring
ones: the wrapper expands to a single instruction with a trailing
semicolon, which GCC counts as two statements, overestimating the size
of the function when deciding whether to inline it.

This is CVE-2026-102699, part of XSA-519.

Fixes: 9a216e92de9f ("xen/arm: page: Handle cache flush of an element at the top of the address space")
Signed-off-by: Leonid Komarianskyi <leonid_komarianskyi@epam.com>
Reviewed-by: Jan Beulich <jbeulich@suse.com>
Reviewed-by: Julien Grall <julien@xen.org>

diff --git a/xen/arch/arm/include/asm/page.h b/xen/arch/arm/include/asm/page.h
index 90100a96dfda..6da4745736a2 100644
--- a/xen/arch/arm/include/asm/page.h
+++ b/xen/arch/arm/include/asm/page.h
@@ -168,7 +168,7 @@ static inline size_t read_dcache_line_bytes(void)
 static inline int invalidate_dcache_va_range(const void *p, unsigned long size)
 {
     size_t cacheline_mask = dcache_line_bytes - 1;
-    unsigned long idx = 0;
+    unsigned long partial_head;
 
     if ( !size )
         return 0;
@@ -178,23 +178,35 @@ static inline int invalidate_dcache_va_range(const void *p, unsigned long size)
 
     dsb(sy);           /* So the CPU issues all writes to the range */
 
-    if ( (uintptr_t)p & cacheline_mask )
+    /*
+     * A cacheline only partially covered by the region has to be cleaned as
+     * well as invalidated, so that data living outside of the region is not
+     * discarded.
+     */
+    partial_head = (uintptr_t)p & cacheline_mask;
+    if ( partial_head )
     {
-        size -= dcache_line_bytes - ((uintptr_t)p & cacheline_mask);
-        p = (void *)((uintptr_t)p & ~cacheline_mask);
+        unsigned long rem = dcache_line_bytes - partial_head;
+
         asm_inline volatile (
             __clean_and_invalidate_dcache_one(0) :: "r" (p) );
-        p += dcache_line_bytes;
+        p += rem;
+
+        /* The region may end within this cacheline, leaving nothing to do. */
+        if ( __builtin_usubl_overflow(size, rem, &size) )
+            goto done;
     }
 
     for ( ; size >= dcache_line_bytes;
-            idx += dcache_line_bytes, size -= dcache_line_bytes )
-        asm volatile (__invalidate_dcache_one(0) : : "r" (p + idx));
+            p += dcache_line_bytes, size -= dcache_line_bytes )
+        asm_inline volatile (__invalidate_dcache_one(0) : : "r" (p));
 
+    /* Partial tail must be cleaned as well. */
     if ( size > 0 )
         asm_inline volatile (
-            __clean_and_invalidate_dcache_one(0) :: "r" (p + idx) );
+            __clean_and_invalidate_dcache_one(0) :: "r" (p) );
 
+ done:
     dsb(sy);           /* So we know the flushes happen before continuing */
 
     return 0;
